# Port scope and evidence

The exact package and engine source commits are sourceCommit and engineCommit
in HANDOVER-MANIFEST.json. Package construction and compatibility results must
come from separate receipts for those exact inputs; this guide supplies no
current pass or failure. The retained Ruby reference is Decko 0.20.0/card
1.110.0; another version or deployed profile needs its own evidence. Final
source-supply, privacy/sharing approval and native POSIX mode acceptance also
require separate review.

The implementation includes card names and inflections, row fetching, rule and
permission lookup, CQL parsing and SQL generation, views and nesting, history,
card saves and events, account/session handling, attachments, assets and
hand-ported mod registrations. The source map lists each TypeScript module and
its Ruby citations, including uncited modules rather than hiding them.

The compatibility contract has four independent categories: persistence,
semantics, rendering and side effects. Passing a render comparison says nothing
about an unexecuted save or notification. The packaged reference harness can
measure these categories on the included synthetic deck. No current aggregate
compatibility percentage is claimed by this package.

The eventual HANDOVER-MANIFEST.json must pin the actual integrated engine and
packaging source. Older handover notes described byte-checker qualification
and numeric-card work at their own checkpoints; those historical review states
are not asserted as current blockers or closures. The frozen package requires
its own admitted checker/source/fixture/settings evidence. A local build or
green subset cannot substitute for that acceptance.

Source present is not supported deployment evidence. SQL adapter source includes
SQLite, libSQL HTTP, D1, MySQL and PostgreSQL; runtime-specific behavior must be
verified independently on each selected adapter. This package's local recipe
uses Node and SQLite only. The intended export excludes hosted-provider
credentials, deployment resource configuration and production data; that
boundary still needs actual frozen-surface privacy review. Native Bun and hosted-provider
acceptance are outside the package smoke check.

Ruby mods cannot be assumed to execute unchanged. The implementation includes
ported TypeScript mod modules and Ruby-related loading/interpreting machinery;
that presence does not establish arbitrary Ruby gem, native extension, Rails or
HAML compatibility. Inspect the loader interfaces and test the specific mod.
The TypeScript mod DSL supports declared synchronous format helpers, view
permission predicates and set-scoped registration. It does not synthesize
every Ruby trait accessor or make arbitrary Ruby method names override
renderer internals. Each engine owns its compiled fallback view table;
registration in another loader is not shared runtime state for that engine.
The complete development suite and private migration corpora are not in this
source snapshot. A missing test or fixture is not a passing test.

This is a review package, not a claim that all engine work has closed. Release
integration and independent attacks can change the release candidate. Regenerate
and validate this exact package after integration; its manifest pins the code
actually included, rather than whichever branch is later called a release.
